  classDef actor fill:#111,stroke:#444,color:#6cf,stroke-width:1px
  classDef contract fill:#0b132b,stroke:#3a506b,color:#fff,stroke-width:1px
  classDef shield fill:#14213d,stroke:#f77f00,color:#f77f00,stroke-dasharray: 3 3
  classDef state fill:#1b263b,stroke:#4361ee,color:#fff
  classDef ok fill:#0f3d3e,stroke:#2a9d8f,color:#2a9d8f

  %% Swimlanes (subgraphs)
  subgraph M[DAO Member (RepToken)]
    direction TB
    M0[use platform]:::actor
    M1[claimReputation]:::actor
    M2[propose]:::actor
    M3[castVote]:::actor
  end

  subgraph G[DAO Governor]
    direction TB
    G1[(Active)]:::state
    G2[(Succeeded)]:::state
    G3[(Defeated)]:::state
    G4[queue]:::contract
    G5[execute]:::contract
  end

  subgraph T[Timelock]
    direction TB
    T1[(Queued)]:::state
    T2[delay]:::contract
    T3[release]:::contract
  end

  subgraph E[Economy Contract]Here are the full specifications for two diagrams. Someone with design or diagramming software (like Lucidchart, Miro, Figma, or even PowerPoint) can use these detailed instructions to create accurate and informative flowcharts.
Diagram 1: Project & Economy Operating Model
Title: The Trustless Project Lifecycle
Objective: To illustrate the complete journey of a project from creation to completion, showing both the successful "happy path" and the full dispute resolution and appeals process.
Key Actors (to be used as Swimlanes):
Author
Backer / Contributor
Contractor
Arbiter
DAO Member (with RepTokens)
DAO Timelock
Project Smart Contract (as a central object)
Economy Smart Contract (as a system)
Process Flow Specification:
Phase 1: Project Setup
Author: Initiates the process.
[Action] Calls createProject or createERC20Project on the Economy Contract.
[Input] Provides project details (name, parties) and stakes their half of the Arbitration Fee.
[Result] The Economy Contract deploys a new Project Contract.
The Project Contract is now in the (Open) or (Pending) state.
Backer(s): Fund the project.
[Action] Call sendFunds on the Project Contract.
[Result] Funds (ETH or ERC20) are transferred to and held by the Project Contract. Project value increases. This can happen while the project is (Open) or (Pending).
Contractor: Agrees to the terms and starts the project.
[Action] Calls signContract on the Project Contract after the cooling-off period ends.
[Input] Stakes their half of the Arbitration Fee.
[Result] The Project Contract transitions to the (Ongoing) state.
Phase 2: Project Execution & Decision
Contractor: Completes the work.
Backer(s): Review the completed work. This leads to a critical decision point.
Decision Gateway: Is the work satisfactory?
YES (Happy Path) -> Go to Phase 3.
NO (Dispute Path) -> Go to Phase 4.
Phase 3: Successful Completion (The Happy Path)
Backer(s):
[Action] Call voteToReleasePayment on the Project Contract.
[Logic] If the total value of backers voting to release meets the backerQuorum (e.g., 70%), the project is approved.
[Result] The Project Contract transitions to the (Closed) state.
Contractor:
[Action] Calls withdrawAsContractor on the Project Contract.
[Result] The contract calculates and distributes the funds:
A Platform Fee is sent to the Economy Contract (DAO Treasury).
An Author Fee is sent to the Author.
The remaining balance is sent to the Contractor.
Author & Contractor:
[Action] Both parties call reclaimArbitrationFee on the Project Contract.
[Result] Their initial fee stakes are returned to them.
Phase 4: Dispute, Arbitration, and Appeal
Backer(s) or Contractor:
[Action] Call voteToDispute (Backer) or disputeAsContractor (Contractor).
[Result] If quorum is met, the Project Contract transitions to the (Dispute) state.
Arbiter:
[Action] Calls arbitrate on the Project Contract, providing a ruling.
[Input] A percentage (e.g., 80) indicating how much of the project value the contractor should receive.
[Result] The Project Contract transitions to the (Appealable) state. The appealPeriod timer starts.
Decision Gateway: Is the ruling appealed by the DAO?
NO (Appeal Period Expires):
Anyone can [Action] call finalizeArbitration.
[Result] The project transitions to (Closed) with the Arbiter's original ruling. -> Go to Phase 5.
YES (DAO Intervenes):
DAO Member: [Action] Calls appeal on the Project Contract. Must have enough RepTokens (projectCreationThreshold).
[Result] Project transitions to (Appeal) state.
DAO Timelock: [Action] Calls daoOverrule with a new percentage.
[Result] Project transitions to (Closed) with the DAO's new ruling. -> Go to Phase 5.
Phase 5: Post-Dispute Payouts
Arbiter:
[Result] The Project Contract automatically transfers the full Arbitration Fee to the Arbiter for their service.
Contractor:
[Action] Calls withdrawAsContractor.
[Result] Receives the percentage of funds specified in the final ruling (minus platform/author fees).
Backer(s):
[Action] Call withdrawAsContributor.
[Result] Receive the remaining percentage of funds as a refund.
Key Interactions & Callouts to Highlight on the Diagram:
The DAO Timelock has a special power: it can [Action] call daoVeto at any point before (Closed) to cancel the project and ensure full refunds.
Show the fee percentages (Platform Fee %, Author Fee %, Arbitration Fee %) as configurable rules read from the Economy Contract.
Diagram 2: DAO Governance Operating Model
Title: DAO Control over the Trustless Economy
Objective: To illustrate how DAO members can propose, vote on, and execute changes to the rules of the Economy contract.
